{
  "dom_id": "step-q90872",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={2}^{x}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)={3}^{x}=-1.$ In fact, ${3}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={2}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
